<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude"  xml:id="chap_dynamical-systems">
  <title>Dynamical Systems</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      A recurring theme in this book is the use of mathematical models consisting of a set of differential equations to explore the behavior of physical systems as they evolve over time.
      Some examples we have encountered are the <m>S</m>-<m>I</m>-<m>R</m> epidemiological model,
      predator-prey systems, and the motion of a pendulum.
      We call such a set of differential equations a
      <term>dynamical system</term>.
          <idx><h>dynamical system</h></idx>
      Dynamical systems play important roles in all branches of science.
      In this chapter we will develop some general tools for thinking about them,
      with particular emphasis on the kinds of geometric insight provided by the concepts of
      <term>state space</term> and
    </p>
    <p>
      <term>vector field</term>.
    </p>
  </introduction>
